.docname {Socket Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Socket | One open Socket.IO connection from one browser tab | Ada's dashboard tab |
    | Room | A named group of sockets; a message sent to a room reaches only them | `building:b1`, `domain:eng` |
    | Event | The name a message carries to the browser | `telemetry`, `devices`, `notification` |
    | Ack | The answer the server sends back to one emit | `{"subscribed":true,"buildingId":"b1"}` |
    | Claims header | `x-gateway-claims`: who the caller is, set by the edge after it checked the JWT | base64 JSON with `sub`, memberships |
    | Domain | An area of an organization a person belongs to | `eng` |
    | Functional core | Code that only decides: no network, no clock, no `async` | `core/relay.rs` |
    | Imperative shell | Code that does the input and output, and asks the core what to do | `shell/server.rs` |
    | Jitter | A small, per-socket change to a time limit, so not all sockets expire together | 787 to 900 s |

Rust, Axum, socketioxide, Redis. Directory `backend/socket`. Role and choices: [Socket architecture](../architecture/socket-architecture.qd).

The only live channel to the browser. It reads three kinds of Redis messages, picks **one** room for each, and sends it there. It stores nothing.

| Redis channel | Written by | Room | Event |
|---|---|---|---|
| `telemetry:filtered:{buildingId}` | `dashboard` | `building:{buildingId}` | `telemetry` |
| `twin:devices:{buildingId}` | `digital-twin` | `building:{buildingId}` | `devices` |
| `notifications` | `notification` | `domain:{domainName}` | `notification` |

---

## Layout

Functional core, imperative shell. `tests/architecture.rs` fails the build when a rule breaks.

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `src/main.rs` | Only composition: health probe flag, bind port 3000, call `serve` |
| `src/core/auth.rs` | `authenticate_claims_header`, `may_read_building`, `Identity` |
| `src/core/relay.rs` | `get_telemetry_delivery_plan`, `get_devices_delivery_plan`, `get_notification_delivery_plan`, `Delivery` |
| `src/core/rooms.rs` | `room_for_building`, `room_for_domain`, `building_id_from_channel` |
| `src/core/session.rs` | `lifetime_for`, `sweep_interval` |
| `src/core/subscription.rs` | `Subscription`, `ack` |
| `src/core/health.rs` | `wants_health_probe`, `health_url`, `is_healthy` |
| `src/shell/server.rs` | `serve`, `subscribe_to_redis`, `consume`, `expire_stale_sockets`, CORS, `/metrics` |
| `src/shell/handlers.rs` | `authenticate`, `on_connect`, `subscribe_building`, `unsubscribe_building`, `deliver` |
| `src/shell/twin.rs` | `BuildingDomains`: asks `digital-twin` which domains own a building, with a cache |
| `src/shell/metrics.rs` | Prometheus registry |
| `src/shell/health.rs` | `probe_exit_code` |

| Test in `tests/architecture.rs` | Rule |
|---|---|
| `every_source_file_is_classified_as_core_or_shell` | No third place for code |
| `the_functional_core_touches_no_io_crate` | `core/` never names Redis, Axum, socketioxide, reqwest |
| `the_functional_core_does_not_reach_into_the_shell` | `core/` never imports `shell/` |
| `the_functional_core_is_synchronous` | No `async` in `core/` |
| `room_names_are_built_only_in_rooms` | `building:` and `domain:` appear only in `core/rooms.rs` |
| `subscription_ack_fields_are_built_only_in_subscription` | The ack shape lives in one file |
| `the_claims_header_is_named_only_by_the_shared_contract` | No literal `x-gateway-claims`; `claims_schema::CLAIMS_HEADER` only |
| `main_only_composes_and_delegates_to_the_server` | `main.rs` holds no logic |

---

## Start-up

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant M as main.rs
        participant S as shell/server.rs
        participant R as Redis
        M->>M: probe_exit_code(3000): started with --health?
        M->>M: bind 0.0.0.0:3000
        M->>S: serve(listener, REDIS_URL, DIGITAL_TWIN_URL, lifetime, shutdown)
        S->>S: metrics::init, every series registered at 0
        S->>S: SocketIo layer, state BuildingDomains, namespace "/" with authenticate
        S->>S: tokio::spawn(subscribe_to_redis)
        S->>R: SUBSCRIBE notifications
        S->>R: PSUBSCRIBE telemetry:filtered:*
        S->>R: PSUBSCRIBE twin:devices:*
        S->>S: tokio::spawn(expire_stale_sockets)
        S->>S: axum::serve with /health, /metrics, CORS, graceful shutdown

- `REDIS_URL` must be set: `redis_url()` panics otherwise.
- `socket --health` is the container health check. The image has no shell or `curl`, so the binary calls its own `http://127.0.0.1:3000/health` with a 3 s timeout and exits `0` on any `2xx`, else `1`.
- On `SIGTERM` or Ctrl+C, open sockets get 10 s to close, then the process exits with `1`.

---

## Connect

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant E as edge
        participant H as shell/handlers.rs
        participant A as core/auth.rs
        B->>E: GET /socket.io/?EIO=4, cookie authentication_token
        E->>E: check JWT, set x-gateway-claims
        E->>H: handshake, x-gateway-claims
        H->>A: authenticate(s): authenticate_claims_header(header)
        A-->>H: Identity {account_id, account_name, domains}
        H->>H: store ClaimsHeader and Identity on the socket
        H->>H: on_connect: CONNECTED_CLIENTS + 1, ConnectedAt = now
        loop each domain in Identity
            H->>H: join room domain:{name}
        end
        H->>H: listen for subscribe_building, unsubscribe_building, disconnect

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        Hd{"x-gateway-claims present and text?"} -- no --> R["reject, connections_rejected + 1"]
        Hd -- yes --> D{"base64 JSON decodes?"}
        D -- no --> R
        D -- yes --> S{"has sub and accountName?"}
        S -- no --> R
        S -- yes --> Ok["Identity, join one room per domain"]

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 3 | `x-gateway-claims: eyJzdWIiOiJ1MSIs…`, base64 of `{"sub":"u1","accountName":"ada","memberships":[{"domain":"eng"}]}` |
| 5 | `Identity {account_id:"u1", account_name:"ada", domains:["eng"]}` |
| 9 | Socket joins `domain:eng`. From now on it gets every notification for `eng` |

- The `client` opens the socket only after sign-in (`autoConnect: false` in `frontend/src/services/socket.ts`).
- The raw claims header is kept on the socket. Step 6 of the next flow sends it on to `digital-twin`.

---

## Subscribe to a building

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as handlers.rs<br/>subscribe_building
        participant D as twin.rs<br/>BuildingDomains::of
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant A as core
        B->>H: emit subscribe_building "b1", with ack
        H->>D: of("b1", stored claims header)
        D->>D: cached(b1) younger than 60 s?
        D->>D: lock the gate of b1, check the cache again
        D->>Tw: GET /domain/b1, x-gateway-claims, 2 s timeout
        Tw-->>D: 200 ["eng"]
        D->>D: remember(b1, ["eng"])
        D-->>H: Some(["eng"])
        H->>A: may_read_building(identity, ["eng"])
        H->>H: join building:b1
        H->>A: ack("b1", Joined)
        H-->>B: {"subscribed":true,"buildingId":"b1"}

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        C{"answer in cache, under 60 s?"} -- yes --> Dm["domains"]
        C -- no --> G["wait for the gate of this building"]
        G --> C2{"cache filled while waiting?"}
        C2 -- yes --> Dm
        C2 -- no --> Q["GET /domain/{id}"]
        Q --> T{"network error or 5xx?"}
        T -- "yes, first try" --> W["wait 50 to 100 ms, try once more"]
        W --> Q
        T -- "yes, second try" --> U["Unavailable: lookup_failed"]
        T -- no --> S{"2xx with a list?"}
        S -- no --> U
        S -- yes --> Rem["cache 60 s"] --> Dm
        Dm --> M{"any building domain in the caller's domains?"}
        M -- no --> F["Forbidden: forbidden"]
        M -- yes --> J["join building:{id}, Joined"]

| Answer | Ack | Metric |
|---|---|---|
| Domains overlap | `{"subscribed":true,"buildingId":"b1"}` | none |
| No overlap, or empty list | `{"subscribed":false,"buildingId":"b1","reason":"forbidden"}` | `socket_subscriptions_rejected_total{reason="forbidden"}` |
| `404`, other `4xx`, timeout, bad body | `{"subscribed":false,"buildingId":"b1","reason":"lookup_failed"}` | `{reason="lookup_failed"}` |

**Example, many tabs at once.** After a deploy, 5 sockets ask for `b1` at the same moment. The first takes the gate and calls `digital-twin`; the other 4 wait on the gate, then find the answer in the cache. `digital-twin` gets **1** request, not 5. The test `concurrent_subscribers_to_one_building_make_a_single_request` checks exactly this.

**Example, cache.** Ada subscribes to `b1` at 10:00:00; `eng` is cached. At 10:00:40 Bob subscribes: no call. At 10:01:05 Carla subscribes: the entry is 65 s old, so a new call.

- An empty list is cached too, for 60 s.
- A `404` is never retried: the building is not there, and asking again does not change that.
- The id is added as one path segment, so `../buildings` becomes `/domain/..%2Fbuildings` and cannot leave the route.

`unsubscribe_building "b1"` leaves `building:b1`. No check, no ack.

---

## Relay

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant R as Redis
        participant C as server.rs<br/>consume
        participant P as core/relay.rs
        participant D as handlers.rs<br/>deliver
        participant B as Browsers in the room
        R->>C: message on telemetry:filtered:b1
        C->>C: payload as String, skip if not text
        C->>P: get_telemetry_delivery_plan(channel, text)
        P->>P: serde_json::from_str into Value
        P->>P: building_id_from_channel gives b1
        P->>P: room_for_building gives building:b1
        P-->>C: Delivery {room, payload}
        C->>D: deliver(io, "telemetry", delivery)
        D->>B: io.to("building:b1"), then emit("telemetry", payload)
        C->>C: telemetry_relayed_total + 1, payload bytes added

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        M["Redis message"] --> Ch{"channel"}
        Ch -- "notifications" --> N{"parses as Notification<br/>with a non-empty domainName?"}
        N -- no --> Sk["skip, relay_messages_skipped_total + 1"]
        N -- yes --> Nd["room domain:{domainName}, event notification"]
        Ch -- "twin:devices:{id}" --> V{"parses as Devices<br/>and buildingId equals id?"}
        V -- no --> Sk
        V -- yes --> Dd["room building:{id}, event devices"]
        Ch -- "anything else" --> T{"valid JSON?"}
        T -- no --> Sk
        T -- yes --> Td["room building:{id from channel}, event telemetry"]

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Every message goes to **one** room | Nothing is broadcast to every socket |
| A notification with no domain is skipped | Without a domain it would have no room, and sending it to all would leak it to every organization |
| A devices message naming another building is skipped | The channel and the body must agree, or dots of one building could reach another |
| The payload is sent as parsed, never changed | `socket` decides where, not what |
| A bad message is skipped, never half sent | The next message is not affected |
| The building id is the channel minus the prefix | `site:b1` keeps its colon. See [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd) |

**Example.** Three messages arrive:

| Channel | Body | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `telemetry:filtered:b1` | a tick | `telemetry` to `building:b1` |
| `notifications` | `{"domainName":"", …}` | skipped, `relay_messages_skipped_total{channel="notifications"}` + 1 |
| `twin:devices:b1` | `{"buildingId":"b2", …}` | skipped, devices counter + 1 |

If Redis drops the subscription, `subscribe_to_redis` logs `Redis subscription ended, reconnecting`, waits 1 s, connects and subscribes again, forever.

---

## Expiry

The edge checks the JWT only at the handshake. A socket that stayed open would keep receiving after the pass expired. So `socket` closes each socket after a limit.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant X as server.rs<br/>expire_stale_sockets
        participant Co as core/session.rs
        participant S as Socket
        loop every sweep_interval
            X->>X: sleep
            loop each open socket
                X->>Co: lifetime_for(socket id, base)
                X->>X: ConnectedAt older than that?
                X->>S: disconnect, socket_sessions_expired_total + 1
            end
        end

- `base` = `SOCKET_MAX_LIFETIME_SECS`, default 900 s, the same 15 min as the JWT.
- `lifetime_for` takes `base` minus a part of `base / 8`, chosen from a hash of the socket id. Same id, same lifetime.
- `sweep_interval` = `base / 10`, at most 30 s.

**Example, base 900 s.** Jitter window = 900 / 8 = 112.5 s, so each lifetime is between 787.5 and 900 s. The sweep runs every 30 s (900 / 10 = 90, capped at 30). A socket with lifetime 800 s is closed between 800 and 830 s after connecting. 1000 tabs opened at once do not all reconnect in the same second.

Socket.IO clients do not reconnect on their own after a server-side close (`io server disconnect`). `frontend/src/services/socket.ts` handles that reason: it waits 3 s, refreshes the session (`authStore.hydrate(true)`), and connects again only when still signed in. The edge then checks the fresh cookie.

---

## Routes and events

| Kind | Name | Who | Does |
|---|---|---|---|
| HTTP | `GET /health` | probe, `socket --health` | `200` |
| HTTP | `GET /metrics` | Prometheus | Text format |
| Socket.IO | handshake on `/socket.io/` | `client` | Checks claims, joins domain rooms |
| Client emit | `subscribe_building` (building id), with ack | `client` | Checks ownership, joins `building:{id}` |
| Client emit | `unsubscribe_building` (building id) | `client` | Leaves `building:{id}` |
| Server emit | `telemetry` | to `building:{id}` | One tick |
| Server emit | `devices` | to `building:{id}` | Device dots of the building |
| Server emit | `notification` | to `domain:{name}` | One notification |

The edge route `/socket.io/*` requires the gateway check, the same as every other gated route. CORS allows only `FRONTEND_URL`, methods `GET` and `POST`, with credentials.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `REDIS_URL` | none, required | Subscriptions |
| `DIGITAL_TWIN_URL` | `http://digital-twin:3000` | Ownership lookup |
| `FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:5173` | The one CORS origin allowed |
| `SOCKET_MAX_LIFETIME_SECS` | 900 | Socket lifetime base |

The port is fixed at 3000 in code.

---

## Metrics

| Name | Type | Counts |
|---|---|---|
| `telemetry_relayed_total`, `devices_relayed_total`, `notifications_relayed_total` | counter | Messages sent to a room |
| `relay_payload_bytes_total{channel}` | counter | Bytes sent per kind |
| `relay_messages_skipped_total{channel}` | counter | Messages skipped as bad |
| `socket_connections_rejected_total` | counter | Handshakes refused |
| `socket_subscriptions_rejected_total{reason}` | counter | `forbidden`, `lookup_failed` |
| `socket_sessions_expired_total` | counter | Sockets closed by expiry |
| `socket_connected_clients` | gauge | Sockets open now |

Every series exists at 0 before any traffic, so a dashboard shows 0, not "no data".

---

## Tests

```bash
just test socket               # unit, no Redis
just test socket-integration   # tests/*.rs against a throwaway Redis
```

| Where | Checks |
|---|---|
| `src/core/*.rs` | Claims decoding; domain overlap; room names; ack shapes; lifetime within 787.5 to 900 s for base 900 and stable per id; relay plans for every case of `schemas/fixtures/notification.json` |
| `src/shell/twin.rs` | Lookup URL, `..` escaped, colons kept; cache read back, empty answer cached; `503` retried once; `404` not retried; 5 concurrent lookups make 1 request |
| `tests/relay_redis.rs` | Real Redis and a stub `digital-twin`: telemetry and devices reach only that building's subscribers; a scoped notification reaches only its domain, an unscoped one nobody; refused subscription gets its reason; unsubscribe stops the feed; no claims, no connection; a bad message does not stop the relay; the relay comes back after Redis drops it; a socket is closed after its lifetime; all metrics at 0 before traffic |
| `tests/architecture.rs` | The layering rules above |

---

## Gaps

- **One copy only.** `k8s/deployments/socket.yml` runs `replicas: 1`, and rooms live in process memory. A restart closes every socket; each browser reconnects, subscribes again (`onReconnect` in `stores/commons/sensorData.ts`) and refetches what it missed. Every copy reads every Redis message, so more copies would relay correctly, but nothing tests that setup.
- **Ownership is cached 60 s.** A building moved out of a domain stays readable for up to 60 s by sockets that ask in that window, and an already joined socket stays in the room until it leaves or expires.
- **A domain room is fixed at connect.** A new membership reaches the socket only after it reconnects.
